# Offline W1-only comparison in the actual public C512 block

This experiment checks whether selecting the existing W1 GEMM variant10 improves
the whole C512 block. It changes no production dispatch or arithmetic. Both
graphs call the same prepared public block with the same input and real weights.

After review and activation:

```powershell
.venv\Scripts\python.exe tuning/benchmark_c512_w1.py --output outputs/c512_w1_paired_v30.json
```

Dependencies are `tuning/profile_c512_public.py` (the reviewed ordered oracle and
bit comparison), existing prepared-block/checkpoint helpers, and the installed
extension. The controller pins and archives source files, the exact extension,
checkpoint manifest, device metadata, and compiled GEMM/residual/branch/cache
policy versions. An installed binary or active-source change aborts the run.

The nine cases are blocks23,47,30 at all three anchors, independently checked
against `Geometry.from_valid` and `graph_schedule`:

| Valid input | C512 H×W | M | Phases for23/47/30 |
| --- | --- | ---: | --- |
| 1280×720 | 24×44 | 1056 | 0/3/3 |
| 1920×1080 | 36×60 | 2160 | 0/3/3 |
| 3840×2160 | 68×120 | 8160 | 0/3/3 |

Block30 retains its required raw Half output. Other blocks request packed output
only. The input is deterministic synthetic published state, while every block
weight is real. The independent composition forces ordered dense/group GEMMs and
keeps every Half seed and E4 publication point; attention is shared with the
candidate. This does not independently reprove attention or invoke the DLL.

The offline intercept is installed on the numerics instance only while invoking
the public block. It identifies W1 by both exact weight and input object identity,
requires W1 to be the first linear, and requires QKV to be the only other linear.
Only the candidate W1 call sees override10. The override is restored to zero
before any branch, residual, attention or QKV work. A `finally` restores the
original method and override on all exit paths. Capture records the resulting
kernels; CUDA Graph replay runs no Python policy logic. Captured role visits and
compiled selector predictions are stored explicitly, with no kernel-trace claim.

Both graphs retain every output from every invocation. The default is eight
calls per graph, reduced equally when the128MiB combined output budget requires
it. Nonempty outputs must have disjoint live storage. The harness replays each
graph before reading capture outputs, checks every output against the ordered
composition, then collects eleven paired rounds in each order. The22 pairs are
randomly shuffled with a saved seed. Fresh CUDA events time only graph replay;
each elapsed value is divided by the common call count.

After timing, every output is checked against a freshly computed reference.
Three input fixtures, including two changed inputs, are then copied into the
same captured input. All retained outputs are poisoned and both graphs replayed
and checked separately. The harness finally restores the first fixture, replays
both graphs and recomputes the full reference. Input and all prepared weights
must remain byte-identical to their expected values.

The default full-block gate requires **more than3% improvement**, both in the
overall medians and separately in each timing order. Every selected case has an
individual result. `scope_complete` is true only for all nine contracts; subset
results cannot stand in for the full requested scope. No policy is promoted by
this script. The isolated real-weight scanner supplies the policy evidence,
and any eventual compiled change requires native reproof under a new journal.

CPU checks cover exact identity/order interception, restoration on exceptions,
baseline override behavior, geometry, balanced random pair orders, and rejecting
a speed gain that exists in only one timing order. GPU execution remains queued
for root after review; this draft contains no performance result.
